{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1794346100000-engineer-cc2-fix-r2.muse-wonder-1.0.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"Created-GMT: 2026-11-10 21:51:52 GMT\nCreated-Local: 2026-11-11 04:51:52 +0700\nCoding-Agent: muse\nSession-ID: 8bc98c89-c085-48d9-8d6b-77ebd8d9ab54\n\n# cc2 fix round r2 — reconcile the astra review (2×P0, 2×P1, 1×P2)\n\nWorktree: `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc2`, branch `cc2-transform-session-c1` @ `4cfa609d`, left dirty, nothing committed. All five astra findings (REQUEST CHANGES) fixed. Bounds: round-1's five files plus `src/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc` (one forwarding line only); no forbidden file touched (`vm/macro.cljc`, `vm/semantic/*`, `yang/clojure.cljc`, `core/prelude.cljc`, `yin/vm/linker/dht.cljc` untouched). Origins never minted; canonical bytes/addresses unchanged; legacy no-C1 paths unchanged.\n\n## Per-finding disposition\n\n### 1. P0 — safepoint exit-hook correspondence (was safepoint.cljc:261)\n\nFixed. [src/cljc/yang/safepoint.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc2/src/cljc/yang/safepoint.cljc:262): `track-occurrences` now computes the wrapped body's operand index as `(count (:operands exit))` — hook construction (`hook-application`, line 135) prepends the mark's literals, so for `(h/return :tag (f))` the body sits at operand 1, not the hardcoded 0 — in both the exit-only (`[3 [3 body-operand]]`) and entry+exit (`[3 2 3 [3 body-operand]]`) branches. Verification: `derive-call-sites` (line 322, 348) checks every mapped output path against the derived tree's own `vm/application-occurrences`; a place that is not an application there (including an untracked/nil place) is refused as `{:kind :unmapped :reason :output-not-an-application}` with no row emitted, and injected places are likewise filtered to verified applications (line 365) with unverified ones recorded `:unmapped`. Tests: `safepoint-exit-hook-c1-test` (test line 1560) covers exit-only, entry+exit, and nested marks, computing the wrong coordinate `[3 [3 0]]` independently from canonical rows (it is the `:literal`, no row claims it) against the correct `[3 [3 1]]`.\n\n### 2. P0 — publish proof borrowed across admissions (was publish.cljc:568)\n\nFixed. [src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc2/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc:559): `proven-call-sites?` (which ignored the key origin) is replaced by `retained-call-site-rows`, resolving proof by the indexed program's actual admission + root — the same `[admission-origin final-root]` identity the expander keys tables by: the index transaction `t` and the admitting expansion's source-batch counter both number the session's program stream in admission order, so a program's own table is exactly the entry keyed `[[:source _ t _] root]`. No exact match (stale admission, drifted counters, distinct admission of identical content) → association unavailable → recovery refused (claim-free publication, as before). A matched table must still prove itself: five-coordinate rows, `vm/occurrence-origin?` origins, version 1, unique ownership, exact coverage. Per-occurrence history: `module-from-index` (line 785) shifts each proven program's retained rows into the assembled tree's coordinates via `sequenced-prefixes` and carries them on the spec's `:call-site-rows` with their own origins intact; `recovered-call-site-claim` (line 621), wired into `publish-closure!` (line 289), publishes those rows verbatim and claims only the remaining eligible applications — the synthesized `sequenced` wrappers — under the publication admission origin. Tests: updated `module-from-index-c1-test` (published rows keep retained origins, wrappers separately claimed, every retained occurrence survives) and new `module-from-index-c1-adversarial-test` (line 1680: stale-admission rekeyed tables, wrong-version, incomplete — all publish claim-free, positively asserted via absent markers, absent `:call-site-rows`, absent `:call-sites`).\n\n### 3. P1 — encoder projected-claim shape (was encoder.cljc:316)\n\nFixed. [src/cljc/yin/vm/encoder.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc2/src/cljc/yin/vm/encoder.cljc:308): `check-projected-claim!` now validates the full row shape before coverage — exactly five coordinates (`:yang/call-site-malformed`), origin via `vm/occurrence-origin?` (`:yang/call-site-invalid-origin`; the review's `[[nil R [] :yang/call-site 1]]` now refuses), version 1 (`:yang/call-site-unsupported-version`), no duplicate rows (`:yang/call-site-duplicate-entry`), no two origins claiming one application (`:yang/call-site-multi-claim`), then exact `[root path]` coverage as before. Rule vocabulary mirrors `yang.clojure/validate-call-site-coverage!`. Tests: `encoder-projected-claim-shape-test` (line 1653) plus the round-1 incomplete/version cases.\n\n### 4. P1 — REPL publication forwarding (was repl.cljc:1317)\n\nFixed within the granted extension. [src/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc2/src/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc:815): exactly one line — `link-answer` now forwards `:call-site-tables (:call-site-tables ctx)` into the `publish-name!` opts, so ordinary `yin.link/publish` carries the retained tables (the round-1 `link-context` already supplied them). Test: `repl-query-link-forwarding-test` (line 1727) captures the opts `publish-name!` receives through `link-answer` and asserts the tables arrive. **cc3 item (out of scope, not touched):** `yin/vm/linker/dht.cljc` `publish-name!` (~line 70) returns `select-keys [:address :links]`, discarding the publication result's `:call-sites` side-record handle, and a bare manifest cannot discover it (`published-call-site-table` needs the record address) — custody through the DHT/session path stays incomplete until cc3 retains/exposes that handle.\n\n### 5. P2 — adversarial tests\n\nFixed. Five new deftests in [test/yang/clojure_callsite_test.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc2/test/yang/clojure_callsite_test.cljc:1560) (28 tests total, was 23): exit-hook cases above; stale-admission / wrong-version / incomplete index tables; invalid injected-origin (`:origin` refusal) and invalid projected origin; and the safepoint refusal asserted by the POSITIVE `:unmapped` entry (`{:kind :unmapped :input [...] :reason :output-not-an-application}`, line 1620) — not by absence of a surviving row — with the forbidden output coordinate `[3 [3 0]]` computed independently from canonical rows (tagged `:literal`). The new tests were run before the fixes: 17 failures + 1 error, reproducing every finding (including the review's exact wrong coordinate), then green after.\n\n## Verification (all foreground, explicit timeouts, nothing backgrounded)\n\n| Command | Outcome |\n|---|---|\n| `clojure -M:test -n yang.clojure-callsite-test` (pre-fix, new tests only) | 28 tests, 312 assertions, 17 failures, 1 error (authentic reproduction) |\n| `clojure -M:test -n yang.clojure-callsite-test` (final) | **28 tests, 313 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors** (round-1 final was 23/285; +5 tests) |\n| `clojure -M:test -n yang.safepoint-test -n yin.vm.linker.publish-test` | **24 tests, 102 assertions, 0 failures** |\n| `clojure -M:test -n yin.repl.query-test` | **24 tests, 351 assertions, 0 failures** |\n| `clj -M:kondo --lint` (all 6 changed files) | **0 errors**, 1 warning — the pre-existing `load` refers warning in encoder.cljc (exempt per brief) |\n\n## Unverified / notes\n\n- The kit contract test was not run (orchestrator runs it separately, per brief).\n- Association caveat (fail-closed by design): when the expander's source-batch counter and the index transaction counter drift apart (e.g. a failed expansion consumes a batch but indexes nothing, or a rehydrated index meets a fresh expander), `retained-call-site-rows` finds no exact `[[:source _ t _] root]` match and recovery is refused — the module publishes claim-free rather than borrowing a table. This is the brief's \"refuse recovery when that association is unavailable,\" applied conservatively.\n- `repl-query-link-forwarding-test` stubs `linker.dht/publish-name!` to capture opts (the DHT publish itself is covered by the existing dht/publish suites, not re-run here); the end-to-end `yin.link/publish` claim then flows through the unchanged `dht/publish-name!` → `module-from-index` path proven by `module-from-index-c1-test`.\n- The untracked `CC2-REVIEW.md` in the worktree predates this round and was left untouched.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
