    ;; reason is the unsupported version, never incomplete coverage\n   310\t      (and claim (contains? claim :unsupported-version))\n   311\t      (refusal :yin.link.publish/unsupported-call-site-version claim)\n   312\t\n   313\t      (and claim (not (:rows claim)))\n   314\t      (refusal :yin.link.publish/incomplete-call-site-coverage claim)\n   315\t\n   316\t      :else\n   317\t      (let [tree-address (vm/materialize-tree! handle tree)\n   318\t            sem-vector (:vector (linearize/project-rows tree))\n    45\t(defn publish!\n    46\t  \"`publish-module!` on the node's store: the blobs join the node's\n    47\t   current publication window, reported by the announcement that closes\n    48\t   it.\"\n    49\t  [node spec]\n    50\t  (publish/publish-module! (dht/store node) spec))\n    51\t\n    52\t\n    53\t(defn publish-name!\n    54\t  \"Publish the module `name` exporting `exports`, derived from the\n    55\t   publisher's own index `db` (`module-from-index`, section 5.3), through\n    56\t   the node's store, and sign what binds the name to it (6.6): answers\n    57\t   `{:status :ok :address a :links {...} :envelopes [...]}`, each envelope\n    58\t   `publish/assertion`'s answer, for the caller to enter into its index\n    59\t   as one transaction (6.1) before it announces.  Without `key` it is\n    60\t   refused `:yin.link.publish/no-key`; any refusal writes nothing.\n    61\t   `opts` also carries `module-from-index`'s `:primitives`, `:modules`\n    62\t   and `:host-modules`.\"\n    63\t  [node db {:keys [key name] :as opts}]\n    64\t  (if-not key\n    65\t    (refused :yin.link.publish/no-key {:name name})\n    66\t    (let [spec (publish/module-from-index db opts)\n    67\t          res (if (= :refused (:status spec)) spec (publish! node spec))]\n    68\t      (if (= :refused (:status res))\n    69\t        res\n    70\t        (assoc (select-keys res [:address :links])\n    71\t               :status :ok\n    72\t               :envelopes (publish/name-envelopes db key name (:address res)))))))\n    73\t\n    74\t\n10:- **P0 | [publish.cljc:568](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc2/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc:568) | Recovery borrows proof across distinct admissions of identical content.** `proven-call-sites?` explicitly ignores `_origin` and accepts any table matching the root and application paths. Admission A can claim root R, followed by unclaimed admission B producing R; publishing B then accepts A’s retained table. This defeats the distinction expressly retained by [macro/call-site-table:1849](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc2/src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc:1849). [Module assembly:694](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc2/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc:694) subsequently replaces the evidence with blanket markers, retaining no old/new occurrence correspondence. **Required fix:** resolve proof using the indexed program’s actual admission plus root, refusing recovery when that association is unavailable. Preserve the selected occurrence history through assembly; claim synthesized wrappers separately under publication admission.\n12:- **P1 | [encoder.cljc:316](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc2/src/cljc/yin/vm/encoder.cljc:316) | Projected claims accept malformed origins and conflicting ownership.** For a tree whose sole application is its root, `[[nil R [] :yang/call-site 1]]` passes: validation checks version and `[root path]` coverage but never origin validity. Duplicate rows, extra row fields, and two origins claiming the same application also survive because coverage is reduced to a set. [project-member:123](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc2/src/cljc/yin/vm/encoder.cljc:123) then forwards them unchanged. **Required fix:** validate complete row shape, ruled origins, and unique occurrence ownership, alongside exact coverage and version.\n14:- **P1 | [repl.cljc:1317](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc2/src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:1317) | Normal REPL publication remains disconnected.** Tables enter `link-context`, but [query/link-answer:810](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc2/src/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc:810) constructs publication options without them. Consequently ordinary `yin.link/publish` still publishes claim-free. Forwarding alone also leaves custody incomplete: [dht/publish-name!:70](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc2/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/dht.cljc:70) discards `:call-sites`, whose record is deliberately undiscoverable from a bare manifest ([publish.cljc:438](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc2/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc:438)). **Required fix:** complete table forwarding and retain/expose the publication side-record handle through the session path. Coordinate the necessary ownership extension.\nsrc/cljc/yang/safepoint.cljc\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl.cljc\nsrc/cljc/yin/repl/query.cljc\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/encoder.cljc\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc\ntest/yang/clojure_callsite_test.cljc\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"agent_message","text":"The new publication path has another acceptance hole: it labels every uncovered application as a synthesized wrapper. Removing a retained user-call row from a recovered spec therefore causes that call to receive the publication origin instead of triggering refusal; even an empty `:call-site-rows` value takes this path.\n\nThe encoder’s original acceptance holes are fixed. The forwarding line is also correct within the stated cc2 scope, with DHT custody still deferred to cc3."}}
